# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cecil-native-arm64-runner — a native arm64 machine joined the fleet, and no job asks for it
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: namespace-storage-blocks-ci
#+BRANCH: -

* What happened

On 2026-08-21 pjb registered two runners on *cecil*, an NVIDIA DGX Spark
running Ubuntu on arm64:

: 55390921  cecil shell    tags: linux, arm64, shell, nvidia     run_untagged=true
: 55390973  cecil docker   tags: linux, arm64, docker, nvidia    run_untagged=true

(As first registered they were tagged =aarch64=, and the shell one carried
no executor tag; pjb retagged both to the convention the same afternoon, in
response to this ticket.)

cecil is the fleet's first *native Linux arm64* machine. =release:linux:arm64=
is built on poseidon under *qemu-user emulation* today, which is the slowest
lane the project has.

* Why nothing runs on it yet

Runners are selected by =<os>,<arch>,<executor>= tags, and every job in
=.gitlab-ci.yml= names its tags explicitly. cecil's tags now match the
convention, so the configuration *could* dispatch to it — but nothing asks
for it: =release:linux:arm64= requests =linux,arm64,qemu= (poseidon's
emulated lane), and no job in the project requests =linux,arm64,docker= or
=linux,arm64,shell=. cecil is online and idle.

That both runners accept untagged jobs is harmless: there is no untagged job
in this project to mis-route onto the wrong architecture. (It would stop
being harmless the day someone adds a job that overrides =tags:= with an
empty list — the =default:= block otherwise supplies =linux,amd64,docker=.)

* Step 1 — DONE 2026-08-21: the runners were retagged

pjb retagged both to =linux,arm64,{shell,docker}= (=nvidia= kept beside them
as a capability tag, like =cad= / =gui=), so nothing in this repository has
to learn a second spelling for the same architecture. The alternative would
have been a second lane plus a switch — tag sets are AND-ed, there is no
"either tag" operator — i.e. carrying the deviation forever in the file that
documents the convention.

* Step 2 — move release:linux:arm64 off qemu

=release:linux:arm64= can now be pointed at cecil. The job body needs *no
change*: it already pulls =$ARM64_BASE=, the digest-pinned arm64
=buildpack-deps=, which is a native pull on cecil rather than a binfmt one.

The obstacle is that cecil is *intermittent*, like thalassa and the windows
PC, and the parent pipeline *cannot* gate on runner availability — GitLab
evaluates =rules:= at pipeline creation, before =detect:runners= has run
(the lesson of release-1.8.43, recorded at the top of =.gitlab-ci.yml=).
So the choice must be explicit, in the established style of
=SKIP_WINDOWS_RELEASE= / =ARM32_RELEASE=: keep qemu-on-poseidon as the
always-on default, and let a CI variable send the lane to cecil. Flip the
default the day cecil has proved itself over a few releases.

Worth measuring before and after: the emulated lane's duration is the whole
justification.

*Not done yet, deliberately.* GitLab expands variables inside =tags:=, so the
switch is a one-line change — and that one line is on the RELEASE path, which
cannot be exercised at all while the namespace is over quota
(namespace-storage-blocks-ci.issue). A mis-expanded tag would leave the lane
matching no runner and silently drop arm64 from a release: the exact shape of
the three faults that made 1.9.0's Windows products unusable. It goes in when
a pipeline can run it.

* What cecil does NOT solve

*arm32.* The DGX Spark's Grace cores are AArch64-only — the Cortex-X925 /
A725 generation dropped AArch32 at EL0 — so cecil cannot run armv7 code
natively any more than poseidon can. =release-arm32-thread-less-sbcl.issue=
still needs real 32-bit hardware (a Pi), or a threaded armhf SBCL.
This is reasoning from the CPU generation, not a probe: confirm it on the
machine (=cat /proc/cpuinfo=; try a =linux/arm/v7= container) before acting
on it either way.
